 "source": "HARMAN technology Limited, ILFORD MULTIGRADE RC PAPERS Technical Information (June 2019); curves traced from the PDF's raster chart images (spectral page 1, characteristic curves page 3)",
 "notes": "Approximate digitization; not supplied or endorsed by HARMAN technology. Charts are embedded raster images (no vector curve paths); curves were traced at native resolution with a centreline follower after the 1 px grid lines, frame, grade labels and leader lines were removed by a 3x3 grey opening; axes calibrated on the printed grid lines and tick marks. Characteristic curves (page 3): MULTIGRADE IV RC Deluxe exposed through MULTIGRADE filters 00, 0, 1, 2, 3 (left chart) and 4, 5 (right chart); developer MULTIGRADE 1+9, 1 min at 20 C/68 F. Half-grade filters are not charted. The text calls these 'typical' curves and says the MG RC papers are broadly similar. x is the chart's 'Relative log exposure' (labels 1-4, arbitrary origin); both charts share the same printed axis, so grades 4 and 5 are on the same x scale as 00-3 as printed (the text notes filters 4-5 need about double the exposure; that is not added here). Density labelled 1.0 and 2.0, zero at the bottom grid line by equal spacing. Where curves overlap (all grades share the toe and the top plateau; 00, 0, 1 and 2 nearly coincide between about D 0.3 and 1.2) they are traced as one shared line. Grades 4 and 5 cross in their steep middle: grade 5 is to the left above about D 0.9 and lower in the toe; the shared part between those regions is common to both. Tracing noise in the flat toe/plateau was removed with a running maximum so density is non-decreasing. Spectral sensitivity (page 1, 'ILFORD MULTIGRADE papers all have very similar spectral sensitivity'): the chart has a wavelength axis only and no sensitivity scale; values are the curve height above the chart's bottom frame divided by the peak height (peak = 1.0, frame bottom = 0.0); whether the original is linear or log is not stated. Not recorded: ISO range and ISO speed tables (page 2).",
